course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 15
title: Football, wait, let's play and bagsy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The best way into a group of kids anywhere is a ball, and the best Ukrainian sentence for it is mozhna z vAmy? — can I play with you? — usually answered with daVAI!
explain: Then the pitch fills with shouts: daI pas! (pass it to me!), shvYdshe! (faster!), and hOL! — goal! — with a soft, breathy h.
explain: stIi! (stop!) and zachekAi! (wait!, hang on!) matter off the pitch too, for example when the ball rolls towards the road.
explain: Claiming things has its own magic word, chur!, like «bagsy!» or «dibs!»: chur meNE! is «bags me!» and chur ne ya! is «not me!».
fact: futbOl means football, the game the friends play in the park; futbolka, which sounds similar, is a T-shirt.
fact: hOL! means goal!, shouted when someone scores; the h is breathy and soft.
fact: daI pas! means pass it to me!
fact: stIi! means stop! or wait!, in games and when crossing roads.
fact: zachekAi! means wait!, hang on!, asking a friend to wait; chekai is also heard.
fact: shvYdshe! means faster! or hurry up!, shouted in races.
fact: hrAimo! means let's play!, from hraty, to play, and the answer is often daVAI!
fact: mozhna z vAmy? means can I play with you?, a very useful sentence for joining a group.
fact: ya v hrI means I'm in, I'm in the game.
fact: chur! means bagsy! or dibs!, or calls a time-out: chur meNE! is bags me! and chur ne ya! is not me!
fact: nIchyia means a draw, a tied game.
fact: pishlI na mAidanchyk means let's go to the playground; maidanchyk is a little square, and Maidan is also the famous square in Kyiv.
exercise: What is futbOl? | Football (soccer) | A T-shirt | A football boot | A basketball
exercise: What is a futbolka? | A T-shirt | A football | A football fan | A goalkeeper
exercise: What do you shout when someone scores? | hOL! | stIi! | chur! | nIchyia!
exercise: What does daI pas! mean? | Pass it to me! | Give me five! | Go away! | Pass the salt!
exercise: What does stIi! mean? | Stop! | Steal it! | Stand up! | Shoot!
exercise: What does zachekAi! mean? | Wait! hang on! | Catch it! | Check it, look! | Hurry up!
exercise: What does shvYdshe! mean? | Faster! hurry up! | Slower! take it easy! | Shoot! | Swap!
exercise: What does hrAimo! mean? | Let's play! | Let's eat! | Let's go home! | Let's stop!
exercise: What does mozhna z vAmy? mean? | Can I play with you? | Can I have your ball? | Can you come to my house? | Can I go first?
exercise: What is a usual answer to mozhna z vAmy? | daVAI! | nE-a, BUvai | ya nayIvsya | zachekAi, proBAch
exercise: What does ya v hrI mean? | I'm in (the game) | I'm out of the game | I'm hungry | I'm hiding
exercise: What does chur! mean? | Bagsy! dibs! or time out! | Cheers! good health to you! | Church! | Catch!
exercise: What does chur ne ya! mean? | Not me! | Bags me! | Me first! | My ball!
exercise: What is nIchyia? | A draw, a tied game | A night game under lights | A win | A penalty
exercise: What does pishlI na mAidanchyk mean? | Let's go to the playground | Let's go to Kyiv | Let's go to the shop for sweets | Let's go to bed
exercise: Besides playground, what is Maidan known as? | A famous square in Kyiv | A football club from Kyiv | A river | A mountain
test: «futbolka, that's a football, chuck it here» Which is right? | No, a futbolka is a T-shirt; the ball is m'yach | Yes, it's the ball | Yes, it's a goal | No, it's a football boot
test: The ball rolls towards the road and your friend runs after it. What do you shout? | stIi! | hOL! | daI pas! | chur!
test: You want to join a match in the park. What do you ask? | mozhna z vAmy? | ty vOdysh? | chur meNE? | ya proHRAv?
test: The score ends dva–dva. What is it? | nIchyia | hOL | ya vyhRAv | ODyn-nol
test: Someone asks who'll fetch the ball from the nettles. What do the clever kids shout fast? | chur ne ya! | chur meNE! | daI pas! | hOL!
test: There's one last ice lolly. What do you shout to claim it? | chur meNE! | chur ne ya! | zachekAi! | nIchyia!
test: You're tying your shoelace and the others run off. What do you shout? | zachekAi! | shvYdshe! | hrAimo! | hOL!
test: What would happen if you shouted «shvYdshe!» to your slow teammate in a race? | He'd know you want him to hurry up | He'd stop at once | He'd pass you the ball | He'd think you scored
test: «hOL, with a hard h like in hot» What is right? | The h is soft and breathy | It's a hard k sound, «kol» | The h is silent, «ol» | It's said «gol» like Spanish
test: Sashko says «hrAimo!» and his brother answers «daVAI! ya v hrI». What did the brother say? | Come on! I'm in | No, I'm out | Wait, I'm hungry | Stop, it's a draw
test: «pishlI na mAidanchyk» means go to the famous Maidan in Kyiv, right? | No, here it's just the local playground | Yes, all the way to Kyiv, the big square | Yes, it's a train | No, it's the beach
test: «oi daI pas, I'm free!» What should you do? | Pass him the ball | Give him a high five | Stop the game at once | Take a break for water
exam: Identify the utterance by which a child requests admission to a game already in progress. | mozhna z vAmy? | chur ne ya! | ty proHRAv | nIchyia
exam: Select the response consistent with the established signification of futbolka. | It denotes a T-shirt, notwithstanding its resemblance to futbOl | It denotes a football | It denotes a football supporter | It denotes the referee
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the exclamation chur!? | It is employed to lay claim to something or to call a suspension of play | It announces the scoring of a goal | It signifies a draw | It is an expression of gratitude
exam: Ascertain the result designated by nIchyia. | A drawn game | A victory by a single goal | A game abandoned by night | A penalty awarded
